<div class="tribe-manual">
  <ManualHeader />

  <ManualPageNav @activePage="loom" />

  <div class="container pb-5">

    <div class="alert alert-dark mb-4">
      <div class="row align-items-center">
        <div class="col-md-8">
          <h5 class="mb-1"><i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group me-2"></i>Loom:
            Multi Tribe Hosting</h5>
          <p class="mb-0 small text-muted">One shared services stack. Many
            isolated Threads. Each Thread is a plain Docker Compose stack
            running a full Tribe and Junction instance with its own database,
            uploads, ports, and static and PHP distribution sites.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-4 text-md-end mt-2 mt-md-0">
          <span class="badge bg-dark me-1">MySQL</span>
          <span class="badge bg-dark me-1">Tika</span>
          <span class="badge bg-dark me-1">Caddy</span>
          <span class="badge bg-dark">Docker</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="steps-container mb-5">

      <div
        class="step-card open"
        data-step-name="Loom concept architecture threads shared services compose stack"
      >
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(this)">
          <span class="step-badge"><i
              class="fa-solid fa-diagram-project"
            ></i></span>
          <span class="step-title">Architecture Overview</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down step-chevron"></i>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>Loom separates
            <strong>shared services</strong>
            (run once) from
            <strong>Threads</strong>
            (one Compose stack per tenant). There is no CLI. A Thread is a
            directory you copy, configure, and bring up with
            <code>docker compose</code>.</p>
          <div class="row g-3 mb-3">
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <div class="card method-item h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <h6 class="mb-2"><strong>Shared services</strong></h6>
                  <p class="small text-muted mb-1">An adjacent stack, started
                    once, publishing its ports on the host:</p>
                  <ul class="small mb-0">
                    <li>MySQL — one instance holding every Thread database</li>
                    <li>Tika — document text extraction</li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <div class="card method-item h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <h6 class="mb-2"><strong>Per Thread</strong></h6>
                  <p class="small text-muted mb-1">Started with
                    <code>docker compose up -d</code>
                    inside the Thread directory:</p>
                  <ul class="small mb-0">
                    <li>Its own MySQL database and least-privilege user</li>
                    <li>Tribe, Junction, Dist (static) and Dist-PHP sites, each
                      behind its own Caddy</li>
                    <li>phpMyAdmin, Cronicle and Centrifugo</li>
                    <li>Its own Docker bridge network,
                      <code>docker-compose.yml</code>,
                      <code>.env</code>, uploads, and logs</li>
                  </ul>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="alert alert-light mb-0">
            <strong>How a Thread reaches the shared services:</strong>
            every container in the stack resolves the hostname
            <code>shared-services</code>
            through an
            <code>extra_hosts</code>
            entry, which points at
            <code>SHARED_SERVICES_HOST</code>
            (default
            <code>host-gateway</code>). MySQL and Tika are then reached on
            <code>SHARED_DB_PORT</code>
            and
            <code>SHARED_TIKA_PORT</code>. The two stacks do not need to share a
            Docker network.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="step-card"
        data-step-name="Loom prerequisites shared services mysql tika applications"
      >
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(this)">
          <span class="step-badge">1</span>
          <span class="step-title">Prerequisites</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down step-chevron"></i>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>Before creating a Thread, confirm the following are in place:</p>
          <div class="row g-3">
            <div class="col-md-4">
              <div class="card method-item h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <code>shared-services</code>
                  <p class="small text-muted mb-0 mt-1">An adjacent stack
                    running MySQL and Tika, publishing both on host ports</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-4">
              <div class="card method-item h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <code>applications/tribe/</code>
                  <p class="small text-muted mb-0 mt-1">Tribe application code
                    must be present here</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-4">
              <div class="card method-item h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <code>applications/junction/</code>
                  <p class="small text-muted mb-0 mt-1">Junction application
                    code must be present here</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="alert alert-light mt-3 mb-0">
            Typesense is not part of Loom. Search features that depend on it
            should be left disabled in every Thread.
          </div>
          <div class="alert alert-warning mt-3 mb-0">
            <strong>Publish the shared MySQL and Tika ports.</strong>
            If your existing Tribe installation's
            <code>docker-compose.yml</code>
            is the
            <code>shared-services</code>
            stack, its MySQL and Tika
            <code>ports:</code>
            blocks are commented out by default. Uncomment both before starting
            any Thread, then bring the stack up (or restart it) so the ports are
            actually published on the host:
            <div class="command-block mt-2 mb-0">
              <code>
                mysql:<br />
                &nbsp;&nbsp;## To activate loom, uncomment port lines below<br
                />
                &nbsp;&nbsp;ports:<br />
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- "3306:3306"<br />
                <br />
                tika:<br />
                &nbsp;&nbsp;## To activate loom, uncomment port lines below<br
                />
                &nbsp;&nbsp;ports:<br />
                &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- "9998:9998"
              </code>
            </div>
            Without this, every Thread's
            <code>setup</code>
            service will time out waiting for shared MySQL and Tika to answer.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="step-card"
        data-step-name="Loom create thread directory copy compose env caddyfiles"
      >
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(this)">
          <span class="step-badge">2</span>
          <span class="step-title">Create a Thread Directory</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down step-chevron"></i>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>Each Thread is a self-contained directory. Copy the thread
            template, then name it after the tenant:</p>
          <div class="command-block">
            <button
              class="btn btn-sm copy-btn-block"
              onclick="copyCmd('cp -r threads/_template threads/acme &amp;&amp; cd threads/acme', this)"
              type="button"
            >Copy</button>
            <code><span class="comment"># Replace "acme" with your tenant name
                (lowercase, 2–31 chars, alphanumeric + hyphens)</span><br />cp
              -r threads/_template threads/acme &amp;&amp; cd threads/acme</code>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-3 mb-2"><strong>Thread directory layout:</strong></p>
          <div class="command-block">
            <code>
              threads/&lt;name&gt;/<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;docker-compose.yml<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;.env<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;config/setup-thread.sh<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;config/mysql/install.sql<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;config/tribe/Caddyfile<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;config/junction/Caddyfile<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;config/dist/Caddyfile<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;config/dist-php/Caddyfile<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;config/centrifugo/config.json<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;applications/tribe/custom/ &nbsp;← per-thread
              customisations<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;uploads/
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;←
              user-uploaded files<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;logs/
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;←
              container logs
            </code>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="step-card"
        data-step-name="Loom configure thread env shared services ports database"
      >
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(this)">
          <span class="step-badge">3</span>
          <span class="step-title">Configure the Thread</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down step-chevron"></i>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="command-block">
            <button
              class="btn btn-sm copy-btn-block"
              onclick="copyCmd('cp .env.sample .env', this)"
              type="button"
            >Copy</button>
            <code>cp .env.sample .env</code>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-3 mb-2">Open
            <code>.env</code>
            and set at minimum:</p>
          <div class="row g-3">
            <div class="col-md-3">
              <div class="card method-item h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <code>PROJECT_NAME</code>
                  <p class="small text-muted mb-0 mt-1">Unique per Thread. It
                    prefixes every container, network and volume name.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-3">
              <div class="card method-item h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <code>DB_ROOT_PASSWORD</code>
                  <p class="small text-muted mb-0 mt-1">Must match the root
                    password of the shared MySQL instance.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-3">
              <div class="card method-item h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <code>DB_NAME</code>
                  /
                  <code>DB_USER</code>
                  /
                  <code>DB_PASS</code>
                  <p class="small text-muted mb-0 mt-1">Unique per Thread. The
                    setup service creates all three on first boot.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-3">
              <div class="card method-item h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <code>BARE_URL</code>
                  <p class="small text-muted mb-0 mt-1">Your server's public IP
                    or domain. Used for SSL and for the Cronicle base URL.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-3 mb-2">Then point the Thread at the shared stack:</p>
          <div class="row g-3">
            <div class="col-md-4">
              <div class="card method-item h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <code>SHARED_SERVICES_HOST</code>
                  <p class="small text-muted mb-0 mt-1">Leave as
                    <code>host-gateway</code>
                    when the shared stack runs on this same machine. Set it to
                    an IP or hostname when it runs elsewhere.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-4">
              <div class="card method-item h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <code>SHARED_DB_PORT</code>
                  <p class="small text-muted mb-0 mt-1">Host port the shared
                    MySQL is published on. Default
                    <code>3306</code>.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-4">
              <div class="card method-item h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <code>SHARED_TIKA_PORT</code>
                  <p class="small text-muted mb-0 mt-1">Host port the shared
                    Tika is published on. Default
                    <code>9998</code>.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="alert alert-light mt-3 mb-0">
            <strong>Port allocation:</strong>
            give each Thread its own block of 10 host ports, starting at
            <code>13000</code>. Within a block the offsets are fixed:
            +0&nbsp;Tribe, +1&nbsp;phpMyAdmin, +2&nbsp;Junction,
            +3&nbsp;Dist&nbsp;(static), +4&nbsp;Dist&nbsp;(PHP),
            +7&nbsp;Cronicle, +8&nbsp;Centrifugo. Thread 1 →
            <code>13000–13009</code>, Thread 2 →
            <code>13010–13019</code>, and so on. Generate a complete
            <code>.env</code>
            for any block with the
            <LinkTo @route="env">Env File Maker</LinkTo>.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="step-card"
        data-step-name="Loom start thread docker compose up setup service healthcheck"
      >
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(this)">
          <span class="step-badge">4</span>
          <span class="step-title">Start the Thread</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down step-chevron"></i>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="command-block">
            <button
              class="btn btn-sm copy-btn-block"
              onclick="copyCmd('docker compose up -d', this)"
              type="button"
            >Copy</button>
            <code>docker compose up -d</code>
          </div>
          <div class="alert alert-success mt-3 mb-3">
            <h6 class="alert-heading mb-1">What the
              <code>setup</code>
              service does on first boot:</h6>
            <p class="mb-0 small">
              Creates the upload and log directories and sets ownership to
              <code>www-data</code>
              (UID 33) &bull; Waits for shared MySQL and shared Tika to answer
              &bull; Creates the database, the user and its grants &bull;
              Imports
              <code>config/mysql/install.sql</code>
              &bull; Writes default
              <code>index.html</code>
              and
              <code>index.php</code>
              files for the two distribution sites
            </p>
          </div>
          <p class="mb-2">Every other service waits on the
            <code>setup</code>
            healthcheck, so the stack comes up in the right order on its own. If
            the shared MySQL or Tika does not answer within 60 seconds, setup
            exits and the stack stops. Check that the adjacent stack is running
            and publishing its ports, then bring the Thread up again.</p>
          <div class="command-block mb-0">
            <button
              class="btn btn-sm copy-btn-block"
              onclick="copyCmd('docker compose logs -f setup', this)"
              type="button"
            >Copy</button>
            <code><span class="comment"># Follow first-boot progress</span><br
              />docker compose logs -f setup</code>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="step-card"
        data-step-name="Loom managing threads docker compose start stop logs ps pull destroy"
      >
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(this)">
          <span class="step-badge">5</span>
          <span class="step-title">Managing Threads</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down step-chevron"></i>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>All Thread management is plain Docker Compose, run from inside
            <code>threads/&lt;name&gt;/</code>:</p>
          <div class="row g-3">
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <div class="card method-item h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <h6 class="mb-3"><strong>Thread Lifecycle</strong></h6>
                  <div class="command-block mb-2">
                    <button
                      class="btn btn-sm copy-btn-block"
                      onclick="copyCmd('docker compose start', this)"
                      type="button"
                    >Copy</button>
                    <code>docker compose start</code>
                  </div>
                  <div class="command-block mb-2">
                    <button
                      class="btn btn-sm copy-btn-block"
                      onclick="copyCmd('docker compose stop', this)"
                      type="button"
                    >Copy</button>
                    <code>docker compose stop</code>
                  </div>
                  <div class="command-block mb-2">
                    <button
                      class="btn btn-sm copy-btn-block"
                      onclick="copyCmd('docker compose pull &amp;&amp; docker compose up -d', this)"
                      type="button"
                    >Copy</button>
                    <code><span class="comment"># Upgrade images in place</span><br
                      />docker compose pull &amp;&amp; docker compose up -d</code>
                  </div>
                  <div class="command-block mb-0">
                    <button
                      class="btn btn-sm copy-btn-block"
                      onclick="copyCmd('docker compose down -v', this)"
                      type="button"
                    >Copy</button>
                    <code><span class="comment"># Remove containers and volumes</span><br
                      />docker compose down -v</code>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <div class="card method-item h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <h6 class="mb-3"><strong>Diagnostics</strong></h6>
                  <div class="command-block mb-2">
                    <button
                      class="btn btn-sm copy-btn-block"
                      onclick="copyCmd('docker compose ps', this)"
                      type="button"
                    >Copy</button>
                    <code><span class="comment"># Services, state, published
                        ports</span><br />docker compose ps</code>
                  </div>
                  <div class="command-block mb-2">
                    <button
                      class="btn btn-sm copy-btn-block"
                      onclick="copyCmd('docker compose logs -f', this)"
                      type="button"
                    >Copy</button>
                    <code>docker compose logs -f</code>
                  </div>
                  <div class="command-block mb-2">
                    <button
                      class="btn btn-sm copy-btn-block"
                      onclick="copyCmd('docker compose logs -f php_tribe', this)"
                      type="button"
                    >Copy</button>
                    <code><span class="comment"># Specific service</span><br
                      />docker compose logs -f php_tribe</code>
                  </div>
                  <div class="command-block mb-0">
                    <button
                      class="btn btn-sm copy-btn-block"
                      onclick="copyCmd('docker compose exec php_tribe sh', this)"
                      type="button"
                    >Copy</button>
                    <code>docker compose exec php_tribe sh</code>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="alert alert-warning mt-3 mb-0">
            <strong>Destroying a Thread</strong>
            is manual and deliberate. Run
            <code>docker compose down -v</code>, drop the database from the
            shared MySQL, archive the Thread directory somewhere safe, and only
            then delete it. Nothing reclaims the port block automatically, so
            keep a record of which blocks are in use.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="step-card"
        data-step-name="Loom domain SSL certificate Caddyfile ACME 443 thread"
      >
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(this)">
          <span class="step-badge">6</span>
          <span class="step-title">Custom Domains &amp; SSL</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down step-chevron"></i>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>Bind a domain to any Thread service to enable automatic SSL through
            Caddy's built-in ACME and Let's Encrypt integration. The A or CNAME
            record must already point at your server, and ports 80 and 443 must
            be open in your firewall.</p>
          <p class="mb-2">Replace the site address at the top of the relevant
            Caddyfile, for example
            <code>config/tribe/Caddyfile</code>:</p>
          <div class="command-block mb-3">
            <code>
              tribe.example.com {<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;root * /var/www/html<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;php_fastcgi php_tribe:9000<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;file_server<br />
              }
            </code>
          </div>
          <p class="mb-2">Then publish 443 for that Caddy service in
            <code>docker-compose.yml</code>
            and restart it:</p>
          <div class="command-block mb-3">
            <code>
              caddy_tribe:<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;ports:<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- "${TRIBE_PORT:-13000}:80"<br />
              &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- "443:443"
            </code>
          </div>
          <div class="command-block mb-0">
            <button
              class="btn btn-sm copy-btn-block"
              onclick="copyCmd('docker compose up -d caddy_tribe', this)"
              type="button"
            >Copy</button>
            <code>docker compose up -d caddy_tribe</code>
          </div>
          <div class="alert alert-light mt-3 mb-0">
            Only one Thread on a host can hold ports 80 and 443 directly. When
            you are running several Threads with domains, terminate SSL on the
            host nginx instead and proxy each domain to that Thread's published
            port.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div
        class="step-card"
        data-step-name="Loom junction password thread env restart security"
      >
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(this)">
          <span class="step-badge">7</span>
          <span class="step-title">Junction Password</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down step-chevron"></i>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p>Change
            <code>JUNCTION_PASSWORD</code>
            in the Thread's
            <code>.env</code>, then recreate the two Junction containers so they
            pick up the new value:</p>
          <div class="command-block mb-0">
            <button
              class="btn btn-sm copy-btn-block"
              onclick="copyCmd('docker compose up -d --force-recreate php_junction caddy_junction', this)"
              type="button"
            >Copy</button>
            <code>docker compose up -d --force-recreate php_junction
              caddy_junction</code>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>

    <ManualFooter />

  </div>
</div>

<ManualScripts />
